{"arguments": [
 {"id": "G7", "name": "Gospels are Greco-Roman biographies", "conclusion": "The Gospels should be classified as Greco-Roman biographies, which supports their use as historical sources for Jesus.", "speaker": "Gary Habermas",
  "premises": ["P1: A major doctoral dissertation at the University of London in the last 20 years compared the genre of the Gospels to Greco-Roman bios.", "P2: As a result, the majority view today is that the Gospels are biographies along the line of Greco-Roman biographies.", "P3: This does not mean they are right in everything, but it means they are doing the work of the historian."],
  "strength": {
   "st1": {"v": 2, "quote": "the majority view today is that the Gospels are biographies along the line of Greco-Roman biographies"},
   "st2": {"v": 2, "quote": "a major doctoral dissertation was written at the University of London that changed the view that most critical scholars have in the New Testament"},
   "st3": {"v": 1, "quote": "a major doctoral dissertation was written at the University of London that changed the view that most critical scholars have in the New Testament"},
   "st4": {"v": 1, "quote": "Doesn't mean they're right in everything, but they're doing the work of the historian to start."},
   "st5": {"v": 1, "quote": "When I was in school many years ago, I was taught by a Christian teacher that you don't call the Gospels biographies because they're not."},
   "st6": {"v": 2, "quote": "Doesn't mean they're right in everything, but they're doing the work of the historian to start."},
   "st7": {"v": 0, "quote": ""},
   "st8": {"v": 1, "quote": "All of it early by time of John, still Greco-Roman biography."}
  },
  "prior_art": "Richard Burridge, What Are the Gospels? A Comparison with Graeco-Roman Biography, 1992 (the London dissertation Habermas alludes to)",
  "originality": {
   "or1": {"v": 0, "quote": "a major doctoral dissertation was written at the University of London that changed the view that most critical scholars have in the New Testament", "why": "It is a report of Burridge's prior dissertation and the resulting consensus, not a new argument."},
   "or2": {"v": 0, "quote": "the majority view today is that the Gospels are biographies along the line of Greco-Roman biographies", "why": "No new evidence or data beyond the prior work's genre comparison."},
   "or3": {"v": 0, "quote": "Doesn't mean they're right in everything, but they're doing the work of the historian to start.", "why": "No cross-domain bridge; genre study applied to historicity is the standard move."},
   "or4": {"v": 0, "quote": "", "why": "No formalization, model or structure added."}
  },
  "weakest_link": "The inference from genre classification to reliability: being a bios does not entail the contents are historical.", "develop_next": "Cite the dissertation by name and summarize the specific genre criteria it uses, then show how the Gospels meet them.", "case_map": ["F4"], "domain": "history"},
 {"id": "G8", "name": "Paul is an early, scholarly eyewitness accepted by critics", "conclusion": "Paul provides early eyewitness testimony to the risen Jesus, and even skeptical critics accept that he had an experience of some sort.", "speaker": "Gary Habermas",
  "premises": ["P1: Paul's first epistle, 1 Thessalonians, is dated about 50 AD, only about 20 years after Jesus.", "P2: Even arch atheists believe Paul had an experience of some sort with the one he believed to be the risen Jesus.", "P3: Paul did not make it up or steal the body; most naturalistic theories today are given up by scholars.", "P4: Paul was an eyewitness, a scholar, and a Pharisee trained in carefully prescribed ways of teaching history and facts."],
  "strength": {
   "st1": {"v": 2, "quote": "even arch atheists believe that Paul had an experience of some sort"},
   "st2": {"v": 2, "quote": "1 Thessalonians is only 50 AD."},
   "st3": {"v": 1, "quote": "even arch atheists believe that Paul had an experience of some sort"},
   "st4": {"v": 1, "quote": "He didn't make it up, didn't steal the body."},
   "st5": {"v": 1, "quote": "They often don't define it. He had an experience of some sort with the one that he believed to be the risen Jesus."},
   "st6": {"v": 1, "quote": "They often don't define it."},
   "st7": {"v": 0, "quote": ""},
   "st8": {"v": 1, "quote": "But they like Paul because he's an eyewitness, and he's a scholar."}
  },
  "prior_art": "Gary Habermas, 'The Minimal Facts Approach' / Habermas & Licona, The Case for the Resurrection of Jesus, 2004",
  "originality": {
   "or1": {"v": 0, "quote": "even arch atheists believe that Paul had an experience of some sort", "why": "This is the standard minimal-facts use of Paul's conversion, restated."},
   "or2": {"v": 0, "quote": "Josephus tells us what Pharisees did and they followed carefully prescribed ways of teaching their students", "why": "The Pharisee-training point is a minor addition but not new evidence in the literature."},
   "or3": {"v": 0, "quote": "", "why": "No cross-domain connection."},
   "or4": {"v": 0, "quote": "", "why": "No formalization or model."}
  },
  "weakest_link": "P2: an unspecified 'experience of some sort' does not establish an appearance of the risen Jesus.", "develop_next": "Name the specific skeptical scholars and quote their concession verbatim rather than generalizing to 'arch atheists'.", "case_map": ["K16", "F4"], "domain": "history"},
 {"id": "G9", "name": "Homologia and creeds preserve the earliest gospel message", "conclusion": "The earliest agreed-upon Christian data (homologia) was the gospel preaching about Jesus' deity, death, and resurrection, and it was encapsulated in early, memorizable creeds.", "speaker": "Gary Habermas",
  "premises": ["P1: The homologia is the earliest agreed-upon data in the early church, focused on the gospel preaching.", "P2: Gerd Lüdemann says the preaching of the resurrection message started immediately after Jesus died.", "P3: Creeds were early statements phrased for easy memorization because up to 90% of Jesus' audience was illiterate.", "P4: Examples like 'Jack and Jill' and 'Amazing Grace' show how oral material can be taught to people who cannot read or write.", "P5: The creeds were probably finished by about the time of 1 Thessalonians."],
  "strength": {
   "st1": {"v": 2, "quote": "the earliest agreed-upon data in the early church had to do with the gospel preaching"},
   "st2": {"v": 2, "quote": "the Creeds were early statements about the earliest church that were phrased in a way that could be easily memorized"},
   "st3": {"v": 1, "quote": "Gerd Lüdemann, prominent New Testament scholar, he said that when Jesus died, the preaching of the gospel message actually the preaching of the resurrection message started immediately."},
   "st4": {"v": 1, "quote": "You say it in words they can memorize easily."},
   "st5": {"v": 0, "quote": ""},
   "st6": {"v": 1, "quote": "These creeds are probably finished by about 1 Thessalonians."},
   "st7": {"v": 0, "quote": ""},
   "st8": {"v": 1, "quote": "Gerd Lüdemann, prominent New Testament scholar, he said that when Jesus died, the preaching of the gospel message actually the preaching of the resurrection message started immediately."}
  },
  "prior_art": "Oscar Cullmann, The Earliest Christian Confessions, 1949; Gerd Lüdemann, The Resurrection of Jesus, 1994",
  "originality": {
   "or1": {"v": 1, "quote": "The kernel is the homologia, and it's encapsulated in creeds.", "why": "A vivid variant (sunflower-seed image) of the standard early-creeds argument."},
   "or2": {"v": 1, "quote": "scholars now think that not up to 90% of Jesus' audience were illiterate", "why": "Adds the illiteracy/memorization rationale as supporting data."},
   "or3": {"v": 0, "quote": "", "why": "No cross-domain connection."},
   "or4": {"v": 0, "quote": "", "why": "No formalization or model beyond the metaphor."}
  },
  "weakest_link": "P2: the Lüdemann citation is paraphrased and undated, and 'immediately' is doing heavy lifting.", "develop_next": "Quote Lüdemann's exact sentence with page reference and give the specific creedal texts (e.g., 1 Cor 15:3-5) with their proposed dates.", "case_map": ["K15", "K16", "F4"], "domain": "history"},
 {"id": "G10", "name": "Bart Ehrman grants the creeds are early, Jerusalem-based, and apostolic", "conclusion": "Even atheist critic Bart Ehrman concedes that the earliest creeds came from Jerusalem within 1-2 years of the cross, probably from the apostles, and that Paul knew them.", "speaker": "Gary Habermas",
  "premises": ["P1: Bart Ehrman is the best-known critical scholar in North America and an atheist.", "P2: Ehrman says the earliest creeds came out of Jerusalem, the right place, within 1-2 years after the cross, the right time.", "P3: Ehrman says they possibly or probably came from the apostles, the right people, and Paul probably knew all of them.", "P4: Paul was converted 2-3 years later and then met Jesus, which explains why he accepted the creeds."],
  "strength": {
   "st1": {"v": 2, "quote": "The earliest ones came out of Jerusalem, right place, within 1 to 2 years after the cross, right time"},
   "st2": {"v": 2, "quote": "possibly or probably came from the apostles, right people, and Paul probably knew all of them"},
   "st3": {"v": 1, "quote": "Bart Ehrman, best known critical scholar in North America, an atheist."},
   "st4": {"v": 1, "quote": "And when Paul got killed a plus two to three years later, he was an angry man from here to here, probably because he knew those creeds and he hated the words."},
   "st5": {"v": 0, "quote": ""},
   "st6": {"v": 1, "quote": "possibly or probably came from the apostles"},
   "st7": {"v": 0, "quote": ""},
   "st8": {"v": 1, "quote": "Bart Ehrman, best known critical scholar in North America, an atheist."}
  },
  "prior_art": "Bart Ehrman, How Jesus Became God, 2014 (and his debates with Habermas)",
  "originality": {
   "or1": {"v": 1, "quote": "The earliest ones came out of Jerusalem, right place, within 1 to 2 years after the cross, right time", "why": "Uses Ehrman's concessions as an ad hominem-style variant on the standard early-creeds argument."},
   "or2": {"v": 1, "quote": "possibly or probably came from the apostles, right people, and Paul probably knew all of them", "why": "Deploys the hostile witness's own wording as new supporting data."},
   "or3": {"v": 0, "quote": "", "why": "No cross-domain connection."},
   "or4": {"v": 0, "quote": "", "why": "No formalization or model."}
  },
  "weakest_link": "P4 is a non sequitur: Paul's conversion explains his acceptance of the creeds only if the creeds are already true, which is the point at issue.", "develop_next": "Give the exact Ehrman citation (book and page) for each concession rather than a paraphrase.", "case_map": ["K15", "F4"], "domain": "history"},
 {"id": "G11", "name": "Paul's Jerusalem visit with Peter and James rules out telephone-game distortion", "conclusion": "Paul's visit to Jerusalem about six years after the cross, where he spent 15 days with Peter and James, rules out the idea that the resurrection message was distorted like telephone tag.", "speaker": "Gary Habermas",
  "premises": ["P1: Paul was converted 2-3 years after the cross and went to Jerusalem three years later, about 5-6 years after the cross.", "P2: In Galatians 1:18 Paul says he spent 15 days with Peter and James, the brother of Jesus.", "P3: The theme of Galatians is getting the gospel right; making it too big or too small is heresy.", "P4: Paul went to Jerusalem to get the gospel right and to question the eyewitnesses.", "P5: The Greek word historesai in Galatians 1:18 means to check something out, investigate, question, or ascertain, from which we get 'history.'", "P6: Paul's first question would naturally be to hear the resurrection testimony of Peter and James in exchange for his Damascus road testimony.", "P7: First-century sources exist for the martyrdoms of Paul, Peter, and James; nobody calls them liars or thinks they stole the body."],
  "strength": {
   "st1": {"v": 2, "quote": "It rules rules phone phone tag out because he got it from the eyewitnesses."},
   "st2": {"v": 2, "quote": "He spends 15 days with Peter and James the brother of Jesus."},
   "st3": {"v": 1, "quote": "we have first century sources for the martyrdoms of Paul, Peter, and James."},
   "st4": {"v": 1, "quote": "Paul went to Jerusalem to get the gospel right and to question the eyewitnesses."},
   "st5": {"v": 1, "quote": "You know how long it takes to make a myth? ... I could have dinner with you tonight."},
   "st6": {"v": 1, "quote": "It rules rules phone phone tag out because he got it from the eyewitnesses."},
   "st7": {"v": 0, "quote": ""},
   "st8": {"v": 1, "quote": "The Greek word historesai in Galatians 1:18 means to check something out, investigate, question, or ascertain, from which we get 'history.'"}
  },
  "prior_art": "J. B. Lightfoot / classic Galatians commentaries on historesai; Habermas & Licona, The Case for the Resurrection of Jesus, 2004",
  "originality": {
   "or1": {"v": 1, "quote": "In Galatians 1:8, there's a Greek word historesai.", "why": "A lexical variant on the standard eyewitness-transmission argument."},
   "or2": {"v": 1, "quote": "we have first century sources for the martyrdoms of Paul, Peter, and James.", "why": "Brings martyrdom-source evidence into the transmission argument."},
   "or3": {"v": 0, "quote": "", "why": "No cross-domain connection."},
   "or4": {"v": 0, "quote": "", "why": "No formalization or model."}
  },
  "weakest_link": "P6: the content of the 15-day conversation is inferred, not reported; Galatians never says they exchanged resurrection testimony.", "develop_next": "Show from the Greek that historesai entails interrogating the tradition's content, not merely getting acquainted.", "case_map": ["K15", "K16", "F4"], "domain": "history"},
 {"id": "G12", "name": "Galatians 2 confirms Paul's gospel was affirmed by the pillars", "conclusion": "At the later Jerusalem meeting, Paul presented his gospel to the pillars Peter, James, and John, and they added nothing to him, confirming his message was the same as the eyewitnesses'.", "speaker": "Gary Habermas",
  "premises": ["P1: In Galatians 2:2 Paul says he went up again 14 years later and presented the gospel he preached to see if he had run in vain.", "P2: He calls the apostles the pillars of the church and identifies James, Peter, and John as present.", "P3: Paul says 'they added nothing to me,' meaning his gospel was not deficient.", "P4: In Galatians 2:9 they gave Paul and Barnabas the right hand of fellowship, which is what one does with those one agrees with, not with heretics."],
  "strength": {
   "st1": {"v": 2, "quote": "They added nothing to me."},
   "st2": {"v": 2, "quote": "In Galatians 2:9 they lay hands on Paul and Barnabas. They gave him the right hand of fellowship."},
   "st3": {"v": 1, "quote": "Galatians 2:2, Paul goes up to Jerusalem 14 years later."},
   "st4": {"v": 1, "quote": "you lay hands on people that you agree with."},
   "st5": {"v": 0, "quote": ""},
   "st6": {"v": 1, "quote": "They added nothing to me."},
   "st7": {"v": 0, "quote": ""},
   "st8": {"v": 1, "quote": "Paul, Peter, James, and John."}
  },
  "prior_art": "F. F. Bruce, The Epistle to the Galatians (NIGTC), 1982; Habermas & Licona, The Case for the Resurrection of Jesus, 2004",
  "originality": {
   "or1": {"v": 0, "quote": "They added nothing to me.", "why": "The standard 'right hand of fellowship' argument for agreement, restated."},
   "or2": {"v": 0, "quote": "", "why": "No new evidence or data."},
   "or3": {"v": 0, "quote": "", "why": "No cross-domain connection."},
   "or4": {"v": 0, "quote": "", "why": "No formalization or model."}
  },
  "weakest_link": "'They added nothing to me' shows the pillars did not supplement Paul; it does not show they shared his resurrection content.", "develop_next": "Show that the 'gospel' Paul presented in Gal 2:2 included the resurrection kerygma of 1 Cor 15:3-5.", "case_map": ["K15", "F4"], "domain": "history"}
]}
